# No Component Function Calls in Angular Templates

Do **not** call component methods or non-signal functions directly from Angular templates unless there is explicit justification and approval.

## Why

Methods invoked in templates run on every change detection cycle and can run repeatedly inside `@for`/`*ngFor`, causing performance issues.

## Forbidden in templates

- `{{ someMethod(item) }}` — interpolation
- `[value]="computeValue(item)"` — property bindings
- `[class.x]="isActive(item)"` — class/style bindings
- `@if (hasSomething()) { ... }` — control flow (when `hasSomething` is a method)
- `@for (x of getItems(); track ...)` — when `getItems` is a component method

## Allowed

- **Signal reads**: `someSignal()`, `someComputed()` — these are the recommended Angular pattern.
- **Pipes**: `value | myPipe` (prefer pure pipes for formatting).
- **Event handlers**: `(click)="onSave()"` — user-triggered, not re-evaluated on every CD.

## Preferred alternatives

- **Computed signals** for derived data: `readonly items = computed(() => ...)`
- **Precomputed view-models** (e.g. arrays of `{ label, value }`) built in component.
- **Pure pipes** for formatting only, when reused in many templates.
- **Readonly properties** for static or one-time data.

## Example

```html
<!-- Bad: method called every CD and possibly inside loop -->
<span>{{ dayName(day) }}</span>
@for (a of getItems(); track a.id) { ... }

<!-- Good: bind to precomputed or signal -->
<span>{{ dayNames()[day.index] }}</span>
@for (a of items(); track a.id) { ... }
```
